WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.709
When President Biden took office,

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:03.569
the deficit over the next ten years

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.469
was expected to be about $12.2

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.679
trillion, thanks to a boatload

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.119
of new spending approved during

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.709
Biden's first year, that's climbed

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.929
to 14.5 trillion.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:14.249
One way or another, that shortfall

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:15.749
has to be accounted for by cutting

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.819
spending, raising taxes or printing

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.289
money. So what does the new

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:20.789
Inflation Reduction Act do to

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:21.809
address the problem?

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.579
It doesn't cut unnecessary spending

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.199
or wasteful government programs.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.789
It raises taxes.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.349
And those tax increases will only

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.509
reduce the deficit by about $300

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:32.789
billion or 2%

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.769
of planned borrowing over the next

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:36.389
ten years. In other words, even

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:38.459
assuming no new spending,

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.409
we'd still need about 50 more

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.449
bills just like this one,

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.279
to not add any more debt in the

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.959
next decade. And the bill doesn't do

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.789
anything to reduce the $30

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.709
trillion in debt we've already

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:50.519
accumulated.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.289
Even that small amount of deficit

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:53.819
reduction requires a massive

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.229
corporate tax increase that will

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:56.159
hurt the economy.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.509
There's also a plan to squeeze more

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.219
money out of taxpayers by hiring

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.439
87,000 new IRS

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.329
agents and beefing up the agency's

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:04.229
audit powers.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.639
It's true that this bill is the

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.469
first major piece of legislation to

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:08.489
move through Congress that would

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:10.499
have even a slightly positive

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.389
impact on future federal deficits

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:13.709
since at least the middle of the

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:14.879
Obama years.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.829
But it's not a serious attempt

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.209
to grapple with our coming fiscal

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:19.979
nightmare. The actual driver of

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:21.689
future deficits are entitlement

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.159
programs. Social Security and

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:24.779
Medicare in particular, are expected

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.669
to bring up massive budget deficits

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:27.509
over the next decade.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:28.799
Because they're structured like a

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.599
Ponzi scheme in which today's

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.639
workers are paying the benefits of

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.499
today's beneficiaries as

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:35.999
baby boomers age, and there aren't

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.289
enough working age Americans to

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:38.399
cover what they're owed.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.529
The system is starting to collapse.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:42.719
Yes, $300 billion

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.699
might sound like a lot of money,

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.049
but in the context of government

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:48.179
spending under Bush, Obama, Trump

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.219
and Biden and the exploding

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.049
cost of elder care entitlements,

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.089
it's actually not very much

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.090
money at all.

